<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec_area-vectors" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  	<title>Area Vectors</title>
	


		
    <corollary xml:id="vid-area-vectors">
	<title>Area Vectors</title>
	
	<statement>
		<p>
			
		</p>

		<video youtube="OUbAGPoKYtE"><description>Introducing area vectors.</description></video>

	</statement>
  </corollary>

  <subsubsection><title>Key Ideas</title></subsubsection>
	
	<definition>
		<title>Area Vector</title>
		<statement>
			<p>
				
			</p>
			<p>The <term>area vector</term> <m>\vec{A}</m> for a surface points <em>perpendicular</em> to the surface, as in the figure below, with a magnitude equal to the area of the surface.  For an infinitesimal surface, the area vector is typically written <m>d\vec{A}</m>. An area vectors has units of <m>\mathrm{m}^2</m>.</p>
				<figure xml:id="fig_area_vector">
			<title>Area Vector</title>
		<caption>An example area vector.</caption>
		<image source="elec/area-vector.png" width = "25%" >
				<description>A square with vector A pointing perpendicularly away.</description>
			</image>
	</figure>
		</statement>
	</definition>

	

	<!--
	<p>Area vectors will help us define different surfaces, and will become particularly useful in the next few sections.</p>
	-->

	<subsubsection>
		<title>Activities</title>
		
		
		<activity xml:id="x_area-book">
			<title>Area Vectors for a Book</title>
			<introduction>
				<p>
					
				</p>
				<p>Below is a picture of a closed surface (the outside cover of a book).</p>
		<figure xml:id="x_fig_book">
			  <title>Book Figure</title>
    		<caption>A simple representation of a book.</caption>
    		<image source="Book.png" width = "25%" ><description>A book with LIPA on the spine and Learning Introductory Physics with Activities on the cover.</description></image>
		</figure>
			</introduction>
			<task>
			<statement>
			<p>Which direction is the area vector for the front cover of the book?  For the spine?  How many different area vectors do you need for the entire book?</p>
				</statement>
				<solution>
					<p>
						Each flat surface of the book needs its own area vector.  Since the book has six sides (a front cover, a back cover, a spine, the edge opposite the spine, a top, and a bottom), it has six different area vectors, each pointing perpendicularly outward.
					</p>
				</solution>
				</task>
				<task>
	  <statement>
			<p>Sketch a differential area vector <m>d\vec{A}</m> for a small part of the front cover of the book and write it in terms of differential elements like <m>dx</m> and <m>dy</m>.  Check that your expression has the correct units!</p>
	  </statement>
	  <solution>
		<p>
			The figure below shows an infinitesimal square section of the front cover of the book, where the area vector is <m>\vec{dA} = dxdy\hat{z}</m>.  The units are correct here because both <m>dx</m> and <m>dy</m> have units of length, and <m>\hat{z}</m> is unitless.
		</p>
		<figure xml:id="x_fig_book-sol">
			  <title>Book Solution Figure</title>
    		<caption>A simple representation of a differential area vector.</caption>
    		<image source="Book-Sol.png" width = "25%" ><description>A book with LIPA on the spine and Learning Introductory Physics with Activities on the cover.  On the cover is a small tilted square, aligned with the edges of the cover, with one side labeled dx and the other labeled dy.</description></image>
		</figure>
	  </solution>
	  </task>
      
		</activity>
	</subsubsection>
	
<!--NO AI-->
</section>

